WEBVTT
NOTE how-hot-does-a-welding-arc-get - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.674
A gas tungsten arc welding plasma reaches
approximately eleven thousand degrees

00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:08.994
Celsius at its core, according to
spectroscopic measurements published by

00:00:08.994 --> 00:00:10.651
welding research institutes.

00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:13.411
That is hotter than the surface of the
sun.

00:00:13.411 --> 00:00:18.417
But that figure describes the ionized gas
column between the electrode and the

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:23.809
workpiece, not the metal being joined, and
it holds only under specific conditions —

00:00:23.809 --> 00:00:27.275
argon shielding, controlled current, fixed
arc length.

00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:31.808
Change the gas to helium or increase the
current by fifty amperes and the

00:00:31.808 --> 00:00:34.416
temperature shifts by hundreds of degrees.

00:00:34.416 --> 00:00:38.667
The question is not settled by one number
because the arc is not one thing.

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.709
How hot does a welding arc get?

00:00:40.709 --> 00:00:44.622
An arc is a sustained electrical discharge
through gas.

00:00:44.622 --> 00:00:50.170
When voltage across a gap exceeds the
gas's dielectric strength, current flows

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:55.719
and heats the gas until atoms ionize —
electrons separate from nuclei and move

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:56.715
independently.

00:00:56.715 --> 00:00:58.578
That ionized state is plasma.

00:00:58.578 --> 00:01:03.717
The temperature required to maintain
ionization depends on which gas is

00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:08.771
Argon ionizes at a lower energy than
helium, so an argon-shielded arc at a

00:01:08.771 --> 00:01:13.141
given current runs cooler than a helium
arc at the same current.

00:01:13.141 --> 00:01:17.395
The plasma does not have a single
temperature because it is not in thermal

00:01:17.395 --> 00:01:18.085
equilibrium.

00:01:18.085 --> 00:01:22.357
The core, where current density is
highest, is hotter than the periphery.

00:01:22.357 --> 00:01:26.612
Spectroscopic measurement resolves this by
analyzing emission lines.

00:01:26.612 --> 00:01:30.555
Each ionized element emits light at
characteristic wavelengths.

00:01:30.555 --> 00:01:34.725
The intensity ratio between lines
corresponds to a temperature through the

00:01:34.725 --> 00:01:36.021
Boltzmann distribution.

00:01:36.021 --> 00:01:40.818
This method samples the plasma optically
without disturbing it, and it produces a

00:01:40.818 --> 00:01:43.837
temperature profile across the arc's
cross-section.

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:48.319
Published profiles from the American
Welding Society show the core of a gas

00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:53.220
tungsten arc at eleven thousand degrees
Celsius, the fringe at seven thousand, and

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.088
the shielding gas boundary below three
thousand.

00:01:56.088 --> 00:02:00.868
Gas tungsten arc welding uses a
non-consumable tungsten electrode.

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:05.213
The arc attaches to a small tip area,
concentrating current.

00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:10.115
Gas metal arc welding feeds a consumable
wire electrode through the arc.

00:02:10.115 --> 00:02:14.121
During metal transfer, the wire tip
momentarily reaches nineteen thousand

00:02:14.121 --> 00:02:18.621
degrees Celsius, measured by high-speed
pyrometry at the droplet detachment point.

00:02:18.621 --> 00:02:23.843
That temperature exists for milliseconds
in a volume smaller than a grain of rice.

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:29.168
Shielded metal arc welding, which uses a
flux-coated consumable rod, produces a

00:02:29.168 --> 00:02:32.808
cooler arc because the vaporizing flux
absorbs energy.

00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:38.122
The coating decomposes at approximately
six thousand five hundred degrees Celsius,

00:02:38.122 --> 00:02:40.909
and the gas it releases dilutes the
plasma.

00:02:40.909 --> 00:02:44.990
The workpiece surface does not reach the
plasma core temperature.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.511
Heat transfers from the plasma by
radiation, convection in the shielding

00:02:49.511 --> 00:02:52.211
gas, and conduction where the arc
attaches.

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:57.202
The attachment point, called the anode
spot in direct current electrode positive

00:02:57.202 --> 00:03:01.133
mode, concentrates current in an area a
few millimeters across.

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:06.029
Thermocouple measurements on steel plate
during gas tungsten arc welding show the

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:10.803
anode spot reaching twenty-eight hundred
degrees Celsius, well below the plasma

00:03:10.803 --> 00:03:15.155
core but above steel's melting point of
fifteen hundred degrees Celsius.

00:03:15.155 --> 00:03:20.085
The difference matters because the weld
pool's fluidity, penetration depth, and

00:03:20.085 --> 00:03:24.579
solidification rate depend on the metal's
temperature, not the plasma's.

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:28.796
Argon and helium are the two common inert
shielding gases.

00:03:28.796 --> 00:03:34.685
Argon is denser and ionizes more easily,
producing a stable arc at lower voltage.

00:03:34.685 --> 00:03:40.151
Helium has higher thermal conductivity, so
a helium plasma transfers more heat to the

00:03:40.151 --> 00:03:42.595
workpiece for a given arc temperature.

00:03:42.595 --> 00:03:47.112
In practice, a seventy-five percent
helium, twenty-five percent argon mixture

00:03:47.112 --> 00:03:51.512
increases weld penetration compared to
pure argon at the same current, even

00:03:51.512 --> 00:03:55.091
though the measured plasma temperature
rises only moderately.

00:03:55.091 --> 00:03:59.704
The thermal conductivity difference moves
more energy into the base metal.

00:03:59.704 --> 00:04:04.692
This is how welders adjust heat input
without changing current — they change the

00:04:04.692 --> 00:04:04.941
gas.

00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:09.162
Arc voltage rises with arc length because
the plasma column has electrical

00:04:09.162 --> 00:04:09.789
resistance.

00:04:09.789 --> 00:04:13.491
Longer arc, higher voltage, higher power
for a given current.

00:04:13.491 --> 00:04:17.677
Power is current times voltage, and power
becomes heat in the plasma.

00:04:17.677 --> 00:04:22.618
Extending a gas tungsten arc from two
millimeters to four millimeters increases

00:04:22.618 --> 00:04:27.370
voltage by several volts, raising the
plasma core temperature by hundreds of

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.371
degrees Celsius.

00:04:28.371 --> 00:04:32.989
The published range for this temperature
increase is wide because the effect

00:04:32.989 --> 00:04:37.304
depends on current level, electrode taper,
and shielding gas flow rate.

00:04:37.304 --> 00:04:42.480
Welding procedure specifications control
arc length precisely because this thermal

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.563
sensitivity affects weld quality.

00:04:44.563 --> 00:04:50.114
A longer arc spreads heat over a wider
area, reducing penetration and increasing

00:04:50.114 --> 00:04:53.723
the risk of porosity from atmospheric
contamination.

00:04:53.723 --> 00:04:59.072
Failure isn't an insult to engineering;
pretending failure has been eliminated is.

00:04:59.072 --> 00:05:02.101
Every material has conditions, limits and
a life.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:07.293
The question is whether those limits were
understood and monitored, or ignored until

00:05:07.293 --> 00:05:10.075
the failure became somebody else's
emergency.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:14.214
Spectroscopic temperature measurement
assumes the plasma is in local

00:05:14.214 --> 00:05:18.963
thermodynamic equilibrium — that the
distribution of electron energies matches

00:05:18.963 --> 00:05:21.824
a thermal distribution at a single
temperature.

00:05:21.824 --> 00:05:27.017
This holds in the arc core but breaks down
in the fringe, where the plasma is cooling

00:05:27.017 --> 00:05:27.995
and recombining.

00:05:27.995 --> 00:05:32.190
Emission spectroscopy also integrates
light along the line of sight, so it

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.839
averages temperature through the plasma
depth unless tomographic reconstruction is

00:05:36.839 --> 00:05:37.123
used.

00:05:37.123 --> 00:05:41.280
High-speed pyrometry measures surface
temperature by thermal radiation

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.793
intensity, but it requires knowing the
emissivity of the molten metal, which

00:05:45.793 --> 00:05:49.000
changes with surface oxidation and
temperature itself.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.611
These are not flaws; they are the
resolution limits of the methods.

00:05:52.611 --> 00:05:57.161
Thermocouples cannot measure the plasma
directly because they would vaporize, but

00:05:57.161 --> 00:06:00.307
they measure the workpiece away from the
arc attachment.

00:06:00.307 --> 00:06:05.024
Infrared thermography maps the temperature
field around the weld pool, with

00:06:05.024 --> 00:06:09.176
millisecond time resolution and
sub-millimeter spatial resolution.

00:06:09.176 --> 00:06:12.355
Each method sees a different part of the
thermal system.

00:06:12.355 --> 00:06:16.158
The plasma core temperature from
spectroscopy, the droplet transfer

00:06:16.158 --> 00:06:20.188
temperature from pyrometry, and the
heat-affected zone temperature from

00:06:20.188 --> 00:06:25.013
thermocouples are all real, all different,
and all necessary to describe what happens

00:06:25.013 --> 00:06:25.864
during welding.

00:06:25.864 --> 00:06:30.307
Direct current maintains a constant
polarity, so the thermal load is steady.

00:06:30.307 --> 00:06:34.992
Alternating current reverses polarity
every half-cycle, typically sixty or one

00:06:34.992 --> 00:06:36.915
hundred twenty times per second.

00:06:36.915 --> 00:06:41.524
During the electrode-negative half-cycle,
electrons leave the electrode and bombard

00:06:41.524 --> 00:06:43.523
the workpiece, heating it intensely.

00:06:43.523 --> 00:06:49.220
During the electrode-positive half-cycle,
electrons strike the electrode, heating it

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:49.762
instead.

00:06:49.762 --> 00:06:55.391
Tungsten electrodes in AC gas tungsten arc
welding require larger diameters than in

00:06:55.391 --> 00:06:57.629
DC to handle the thermal cycling.

00:06:57.629 --> 00:07:02.537
The arc temperature itself fluctuates with
the current waveform, dropping near the

00:07:02.537 --> 00:07:06.607
zero crossing when current is low and
rising again as current peaks.

00:07:06.607 --> 00:07:10.019
High-speed spectroscopy shows these
oscillations clearly.

00:07:10.019 --> 00:07:14.370
The progression of measurement techniques
tracks the technology available.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.838
Early spectroscopy in the nineteen
twenties could measure only time-averaged

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:19.603
temperatures.

00:07:19.603 --> 00:07:24.473
High-speed photography in the nineteen
sixties resolved metal transfer events but

00:07:24.473 --> 00:07:25.976
not temperature directly.

00:07:25.976 --> 00:07:29.890
Infrared cameras in the nineteen eighties
added thermal mapping.

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:34.110
Tomographic reconstruction in the
twenty-tens built three-dimensional

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.352
temperature fields from multiple
spectroscopic views.

00:07:37.352 --> 00:07:41.645
Each advance refined the spatial or
temporal resolution, revealing structure

00:07:41.645 --> 00:07:43.565
the previous method averaged over.

00:07:43.565 --> 00:07:48.175
The eleven-thousand-degree figure is not
new, but the certainty about where that

00:07:48.175 --> 00:07:51.805
temperature exists and for how long has
improved substantially.

00:07:51.805 --> 00:07:56.242
The core temperature of eleven thousand
degrees Celsius appears in multiple

00:07:56.242 --> 00:08:00.441
independent studies using different
spectroscopic methods and different

00:08:00.441 --> 00:08:04.227
welding parameters within the gas tungsten
arc welding envelope.

00:08:04.227 --> 00:08:08.361
The consistency across methods strengthens
confidence in the figure.

00:08:08.361 --> 00:08:13.102
The nineteen-thousand-degree measurement
for gas metal arc transfer comes from

00:08:13.102 --> 00:08:17.662
high-speed pyrometry, a different
technique sampling a different location —

00:08:17.662 --> 00:08:20.701
the consumable wire tip during droplet
detachment.

00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:25.484
The six-thousand-five-hundred-degree
figure for shielded metal arc welding

00:08:25.484 --> 00:08:30.138
reflects the cooling effect of flux
decomposition, measured by comparing

00:08:30.138 --> 00:08:33.241
emission spectra with and without flux
coatings.

00:08:33.241 --> 00:08:37.507
These are not competing claims; they
describe different processes.

00:08:37.507 --> 00:08:42.604
The eleven-thousand-degree figure for a
gas tungsten arc core is a typical value

00:08:42.604 --> 00:08:47.127
under standard conditions — argon
shielding, one hundred fifty amperes,

00:08:47.127 --> 00:08:50.568
two-millimeter arc length, pointed
tungsten electrode.

00:08:50.568 --> 00:08:53.533
Change any of those and the temperature
shifts.

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:58.455
Published measurements span nine thousand
to thirteen thousand degrees Celsius

00:08:58.455 --> 00:09:01.357
across the range of common welding
parameters.

00:09:01.357 --> 00:09:05.685
The workpiece surface temperature, the
heat-affected zone temperature, and the

00:09:05.685 --> 00:09:09.569
cooling rate after the arc passes are
separate questions with separate

00:09:09.569 --> 00:09:10.291
measurements.

00:09:10.291 --> 00:09:14.736
Knowing the plasma temperature does not
tell you whether the weld will be sound;

00:09:14.736 --> 00:09:18.125
it tells you the energy available to melt
and fuse the metal.

00:09:18.125 --> 00:09:21.146
Gas tungsten arc welding uses tungsten
electrodes because tungsten's melting

00:09:21.146 --> 00:09:24.166
point of three thousand six hundred
ninety-five Kelvin — three thousand four

00:09:24.166 --> 00:09:27.187
hundred twenty-two degrees Celsius —
exceeds the arc attachment temperature.

00:09:27.187 --> 00:09:31.118
Copper melts at one thousand three hundred
fifty-eight Kelvin, and steel at one

00:09:31.118 --> 00:09:35.000
thousand eight hundred eleven Kelvin, both
far below the arc core temperature.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.835
The electrode tip erodes slowly through
evaporation rather than melting.

00:09:38.835 --> 00:09:43.309
Electrode geometry matters because a sharp
point concentrates current into a smaller

00:09:43.309 --> 00:09:46.824
attachment area, increasing current
density and local temperature.

00:09:46.824 --> 00:09:51.546
A two-degree included angle produces a
narrower, hotter arc than a thirty-degree

00:09:51.546 --> 00:09:53.080
angle at the same current.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.703
The American Welding Society publishes
electrode preparation standards that

00:09:57.703 --> 00:10:01.834
specify tip geometry by current range
precisely because the thermal

00:10:01.834 --> 00:10:05.224
concentration affects penetration depth
and bead width.

00:10:05.224 --> 00:10:10.155
Consumable electrodes in gas metal arc
welding melt continuously, and the wire

00:10:10.155 --> 00:10:14.264
feed rate must match the melt rate to
maintain stable arc length.

00:10:14.264 --> 00:10:18.503
The wire tip temperature during transfer
depends on the transfer mode.

00:10:18.503 --> 00:10:23.105
Spray transfer, which occurs above a
threshold current, produces a stream of

00:10:23.105 --> 00:10:27.101
small droplets at approximately nineteen
thousand degrees Celsius.

00:10:27.101 --> 00:10:32.395
Short-circuit transfer, used at lower
currents, allows the molten tip to contact

00:10:32.395 --> 00:10:35.373
the workpiece, briefly extinguishing the
arc.

00:10:35.373 --> 00:10:40.163
Globular transfer, an intermediate mode,
forms large droplets that detach

00:10:40.163 --> 00:10:40.950
irregularly.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:46.003
Each mode has a different thermal
signature measured by high-speed

00:10:46.003 --> 00:10:50.169
The transfer mode is not chosen for
temperature but for penetration and

00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:54.805
spatter control, yet the temperature
difference between modes is measurable and

00:10:54.805 --> 00:10:55.451
repeatable.

00:10:55.451 --> 00:10:59.298
Aluminum conducts heat roughly four times
faster than steel.

00:10:59.298 --> 00:11:04.235
An arc on aluminum spreads heat into a
wider pool than the same arc on steel,

00:11:04.235 --> 00:11:08.723
reducing peak temperature at the
attachment point but requiring higher

00:11:08.723 --> 00:11:11.544
travel speed to avoid excessive
penetration.

00:11:11.544 --> 00:11:15.760
Copper conducts even faster, making it
difficult to weld without preheating

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.314
because the base metal draws heat away
from the weld pool faster than the arc can

00:11:20.314 --> 00:11:21.213
sustain melting.

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:25.618
The workpiece acts as a heat sink, and its
thermal mass determines how quickly the

00:11:25.618 --> 00:11:27.928
attachment zone cools after the arc
passes.

00:11:27.928 --> 00:11:32.230
Thin sheet metal reaches higher
temperatures faster than thick plate under

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:36.008
the same arc because there is less
material to absorb the energy.

00:11:36.008 --> 00:11:40.510
Welding procedure specifications adjust
current, travel speed, and sometimes

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:44.301
preheat temperature based on material
thickness for this reason.

00:11:44.301 --> 00:11:48.106
The steel anode spot temperature
measurement of twenty-eight hundred

00:11:48.106 --> 00:11:52.637
degrees Celsius, stated earlier, comes
from thermocouples embedded just below the

00:11:52.637 --> 00:11:55.155
surface and sampled at millisecond
intervals.

00:11:55.155 --> 00:11:59.518
The published range across studies is
twenty-six hundred to three thousand

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:02.584
degrees Celsius depending on current and
arc length.

00:12:02.584 --> 00:12:06.406
This measurement captures the peak
temperature, not the sustained

00:12:06.406 --> 00:12:10.933
temperature, because the arc moves and the
metal cools by conduction into the

00:12:10.933 --> 00:12:12.168
surrounding material.

00:12:12.168 --> 00:12:16.385
Infrared thermography shows the
temperature dropping to below one thousand

00:12:16.385 --> 00:12:20.829
degrees Celsius within ten millimeters
behind the arc in a single-pass weld on

00:12:20.829 --> 00:12:21.912
quarter-inch plate.

00:12:21.912 --> 00:12:26.574
The cooling rate affects grain structure
in the heat-affected zone and residual

00:12:26.574 --> 00:12:28.285
stress in the finished joint.

00:12:28.285 --> 00:12:32.982
The arc is hotter than the sun's surface,
but the sun's surface is not the sun's

00:12:32.982 --> 00:12:36.387
core, and the arc's core is not where the
welding happens.

00:12:36.387 --> 00:12:41.215
The metal a few millimeters away is cooler
by thousands of degrees, and that is the

00:12:41.215 --> 00:12:43.368
metal that solidifies into the joint.

00:12:43.368 --> 00:12:47.809
The plasma temperature sets the upper
bound on energy delivery, but the weld

00:12:47.809 --> 00:12:52.308
quality depends on how that energy moves
through the workpiece, and that is a

00:12:52.308 --> 00:12:55.405
question of thermal conductivity,
geometry, and time.
